===Etymology===
{{bor+|en|la|sēpia||cuttlefish}}, from {{der|en|grc|σηπία}}. Cognate with {{cog|it|seppia}}, {{cog|pt|siba}}{{,}} and {{cog|es|sepia}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈsiːpiə/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-sepia.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːpiə|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A dark brown [[pigment]] made from the secretions of the cuttlefish. {{defdate|from 1820s}}
#* {{quote-book|en|
|year=1930
|author=A[loys] Merz; M[orris] Rea Paul
|title=A Dictionary of Color
|publisher=McGraw-Hill
|location=New York City
|entry=Sepia
|chapter=Notes on Color Names
|page=179
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dictionaryofcolo0000aloy/page/179
|text='''Sepia''' had some use in ancient times as a writing ink, and in modern times has has occasionally been used as a pigment, but it never attained any popularity, as it is extremely fugitive.
}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A dark, slightly [[red]]dish, [[brown]] [[color|colour]].
#: {{color panel|704214}}
# {{lb|en|by extension|countable}} A sepia-coloured [[drawing]] or [[photograph]].
# {{lb|en|archaic|countable}} A [[cuttlefish]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}

